WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.747
- Today, I'm going to
talk about the 10 things

00:00:01.747 --> 00:00:04.556
that I did right and wrong

00:00:04.556 --> 00:00:06.223
with my book launch.

00:00:07.203 --> 00:00:09.054
(soft booming)

00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:12.054
(upbeat rock music)

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:18.377
What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:18.377 --> 00:00:19.518
I'm excited to be here.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:20.917
The book has been out for a little bit.

00:00:20.917 --> 00:00:23.306
We've gotten some great
reviews, great testimonials,

00:00:23.306 --> 00:00:25.485
it's been amazing seeing the
results people been getting

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:27.415
by going through the
book, and the audiobook,

00:00:27.415 --> 00:00:28.462
and the ebook.

00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:30.933
It's been an awesome experience,

00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:32.053
and I've been thinking.

00:00:32.053 --> 00:00:34.070
I've been thinking over
the past couple months

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.731
what I did right that really helped,

00:00:35.731 --> 00:00:38.153
that really worked, and what I did wrong.

00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:40.954
So, that one, if I ever
write a book again,

00:00:40.954 --> 00:00:41.998
I've learned a little bit,

00:00:41.998 --> 00:00:43.944
and two, for those of you
who are thinking about

00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:44.777
writing a book,

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.584
these may be some
lessons that you can take

00:00:46.584 --> 00:00:48.760
to avoid having some of the pitfalls

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.455
that I went through in
launching my first book.

00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:53.344
So, let's start off with what I did right.

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:55.571
The first thing was, I
fought for what I wanted.

00:00:55.571 --> 00:00:58.468
When I first talked to
me agent about this book,

00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:00.067
he didn't think it was going to be a book.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.359
He thought it was a work book,

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:03.523
like a little tiny worksheet thing,

00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:05.215
and my literary agent felt the same way,

00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:07.301
that it wasn't really a full-on book,

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:08.655
and I had to fight for it.

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:11.039
I had to prove to them
that it was a full book,

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:13.435
that this is the book,

00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:14.527
right?

00:01:14.527 --> 00:01:16.657
Like, I wrote twice this amount.

00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:19.198
Right, this is 65,000
words, something like that,

00:01:19.198 --> 00:01:20.970
and that's a typical book.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.149
I wrote 120,000 words.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:25.511
I wrote two books worth of content,

00:01:25.511 --> 00:01:27.230
'cause it was so much in there

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.477
that you could potentially
apply your one word to,

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:31.652
and at the beginning, they didn't see it,

00:01:31.652 --> 00:01:32.658
and so I had to show them,

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:33.559
and I fought for it,

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:34.785
and I think a lot of times,

00:01:34.785 --> 00:01:36.595
at the start,

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:38.984
you hear music, there's that
quote about you hearing music

00:01:38.984 --> 00:01:41.947
and so you dance, and the
people who can't hear the music

00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:43.425
think you're crazy.

00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:45.102
You see this person dancing,
you can't hear the music,

00:01:45.102 --> 00:01:45.935
you think they're crazy.

00:01:45.935 --> 00:01:48.049
It's kind of like, what entrepreneurs are,

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.722
is what I was doing at the
start of this book process.

00:01:50.722 --> 00:01:52.994
I had this vision for what I wanted to do,

00:01:52.994 --> 00:01:54.723
and people around me thought it was crazy,

00:01:54.723 --> 00:01:57.088
and so talking about
it more wouldn't help,

00:01:57.088 --> 00:01:58.326
I had to show them.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:00.322
And I was really proud that
I fought for what I wanted,

00:02:00.322 --> 00:02:02.477
'cause I'm really proud of
the book that came out of it.

00:02:02.477 --> 00:02:04.790
So, if you're really convinced
that your idea is a good one,

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.928
and it has to be created, fight for it.

00:02:06.928 --> 00:02:10.582
The second thing is I used a
structure that I wanted, too.

00:02:10.582 --> 00:02:12.915
I was really adamant that...

00:02:13.881 --> 00:02:17.476
I hate reading books that have
long, long, long chapters.

00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:18.940
It really bothers me, right?

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.604
Most books have these crazy long chapters,

00:02:21.604 --> 00:02:23.900
and I would find myself
reading the same page

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.999
over, and over, and over again,

00:02:24.999 --> 00:02:27.578
and I would find myself
forgetting where I left off,

00:02:27.578 --> 00:02:30.229
and I hate closing a book
in the middle of a chapter,

00:02:30.229 --> 00:02:31.760
'cause then, I forget what happened.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.680
I want to end it at
the end of the chapter.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.675
End it on bulleted paragraph, something.

00:02:35.675 --> 00:02:37.865
Some clear demarcation so I know where

00:02:37.865 --> 00:02:39.211
to pick up the next time,

00:02:39.211 --> 00:02:40.513
and so what I wanted to do was be able

00:02:40.513 --> 00:02:43.438
to have a book where every
page was easy to read,

00:02:43.438 --> 00:02:45.058
like the language itself was easy to read,

00:02:45.058 --> 00:02:46.685
but you could pick it up at any point

00:02:46.685 --> 00:02:48.058
and exit at any point.

00:02:48.058 --> 00:02:49.601
And so, if you look, for
those of you have the book,

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:50.683
you know what I'm talking about.

00:02:50.683 --> 00:02:53.198
See like, there's the
end, I could stop there.

00:02:53.198 --> 00:02:55.105
I could flip to the next page.

00:02:55.105 --> 00:02:58.205
Right, there's another chapter
title at the start, right?

00:02:58.205 --> 00:02:59.723
Whatever page you're on,

00:02:59.723 --> 00:03:03.569
there's a clear point to
stop reading if you want.

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:04.402
It flows,

00:03:05.429 --> 00:03:07.890
but there's a clear point to stop reading.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.661
I have quotes at the top
of almost every page,

00:03:10.661 --> 00:03:13.523
and I like that format,
and at the start, again,

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:16.237
my publisher wasn't a fan of it,

00:03:16.237 --> 00:03:19.070
and my way around that was just to

00:03:20.247 --> 00:03:21.185
not tell them,

00:03:21.185 --> 00:03:22.727
(laughing)

00:03:22.727 --> 00:03:24.760
and just keep going with it.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.385
They wanted longer
paragraphs, longer pages,

00:03:27.385 --> 00:03:29.078
and not have so many quotes at the top,

00:03:29.078 --> 00:03:31.148
and I just kept going,

00:03:31.148 --> 00:03:32.714
basically to the point
where we got to deadline

00:03:32.714 --> 00:03:34.329
and it was going to be
too late to change it,

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:36.320
and again, I'm so glad that I did that

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.818
because it's been one of the things

00:03:37.818 --> 00:03:39.379
that people like most about the book,

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:42.181
aside from the content,
which is amazing, right?

00:03:42.181 --> 00:03:44.053
People like that it's easy to read,

00:03:44.053 --> 00:03:45.454
and that you can pick it up at any point,

00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:46.885
and there's a lot of motivational quotes

00:03:46.885 --> 00:03:48.137
and things that tie in.

00:03:48.137 --> 00:03:49.716
A lot of practical examples,

00:03:49.716 --> 00:03:52.306
and so I'm really happy that
I stayed with the structure

00:03:52.306 --> 00:03:54.058
that I had in my mind, as well.

00:03:54.058 --> 00:03:55.619
And this is generally a good lesson.

00:03:55.619 --> 00:03:56.923
If you're frustrated with something,

00:03:56.923 --> 00:03:57.756
you know, I was frustrated

00:03:57.756 --> 00:04:00.756
with the typical reading experience.

00:04:01.619 --> 00:04:03.071
Let me find an example.

00:04:03.071 --> 00:04:04.320
Here's an example.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.153
Here's something.

00:04:05.153 --> 00:04:07.022
Somebody gave me this book.

00:04:07.022 --> 00:04:08.782
Legendary book, said
it changed their life.

00:04:08.782 --> 00:04:11.110
A lot of people talked about
this book, Atlas Shrugged.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.725
Legendary book, and I load it,

00:04:13.725 --> 00:04:15.642
and it's like, look at,

00:04:17.072 --> 00:04:18.155
look at this.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.156
Look at that.

00:04:21.156 --> 00:04:21.989
It's just

00:04:23.051 --> 00:04:25.033
1,000 pages of

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:26.153
intense

00:04:26.153 --> 00:04:26.986
stuff,

00:04:26.986 --> 00:04:29.946
and so I need to prepare
myself to read this book,

00:04:29.946 --> 00:04:33.270
and it's only because it
became so highly recommended

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.905
that it's even still sitting on my shelf.

00:04:35.905 --> 00:04:38.050
Otherwise, it's crazy.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.075
And so, if you're frustrated
with an experience,

00:04:41.075 --> 00:04:43.559
you're frustrated with
reading books like that,

00:04:43.559 --> 00:04:44.862
you're frustrated with anything

00:04:44.862 --> 00:04:46.273
that's happening in your life,

00:04:46.273 --> 00:04:47.241
it creates an opportunity.

00:04:47.241 --> 00:04:48.308
That's what entrepreneurs do, right?

00:04:48.308 --> 00:04:49.493
We solve problems.

00:04:49.493 --> 00:04:51.470
We see a problem,

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.081
and instead of complaining about it,

00:04:53.081 --> 00:04:54.371
we find a solution,

00:04:54.371 --> 00:04:56.034
which is what I tried to do in the book.

00:04:56.034 --> 00:04:58.084
I was frustrated with the
regular book experience,

00:04:58.084 --> 00:05:00.713
and so I created one that
I thought was a better one,

00:05:00.713 --> 00:05:02.728
and thankfully, a lot of you guys agree.

00:05:02.728 --> 00:05:03.894
The number three thing I did right

00:05:03.894 --> 00:05:05.521
was I got a test audience.

00:05:05.521 --> 00:05:08.417
This was another surprising,
really interesting thing

00:05:08.417 --> 00:05:10.942
that is not done in the
publishing industry.

00:05:10.942 --> 00:05:13.943
So, I finished my manuscript,
had 120,000 words,

00:05:13.943 --> 00:05:16.539
and I said well, how do
we split test the titles,

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:18.734
and how do we split test the cover,

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:20.886
and how do we split test the content

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:24.359
to see, right, like in the movie business,

00:05:24.359 --> 00:05:26.429
they'll make a movie,
and then they'll put it

00:05:26.429 --> 00:05:27.682
in front of a test audience,

00:05:27.682 --> 00:05:29.163
and they'll see what their reaction was.

00:05:29.163 --> 00:05:31.359
Did people cry when
you wanted them to cry?

00:05:31.359 --> 00:05:33.837
Were they yawning and falling
asleep in the movie or not,

00:05:33.837 --> 00:05:36.379
and what points did they enjoy, not enjoy?

00:05:36.379 --> 00:05:38.560
When you're looking at
creating a new product,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.428
you go to a test audience.

00:05:40.428 --> 00:05:42.565
Here's a new chocolate bar I want to make.

00:05:42.565 --> 00:05:44.892
Okay, let's go sample it
and see what people think.

00:05:44.892 --> 00:05:46.065
Oh, there's too much peanuts,

00:05:46.065 --> 00:05:48.354
oh, there's too much corn
syrup, whatever, right?

00:05:48.354 --> 00:05:52.599
You test it, you test, you test a product.

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:56.110
Great products come when
you mix what you love doing

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.757
with what the market wants, right?

00:05:57.757 --> 00:05:58.711
The

00:05:58.711 --> 00:06:00.267
business basics.

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:01.562
Yes, you have to love it.

00:06:01.562 --> 00:06:03.480
Yes, you have to see an opportunity.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.568
Yes, you have to create
something from your soul,

00:06:05.568 --> 00:06:07.825
and at the same time, the
audience has to like it.

00:06:07.825 --> 00:06:08.989
There has to be a need for it.

00:06:08.989 --> 00:06:10.472
Otherwise, you're just in a hobby.

00:06:10.472 --> 00:06:11.783
You're doing something that you love,

00:06:11.783 --> 00:06:13.061
but nobody cares about it,

00:06:13.061 --> 00:06:14.844
so you're just often a hobby.

00:06:14.844 --> 00:06:16.123
I didn't want this to be a hobby.

00:06:16.123 --> 00:06:18.759
I wanted this to have an impact,

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:21.659
and in the publishing
world, there is no sampling.

00:06:21.659 --> 00:06:23.150
There is no test audience.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:24.732
It's just not done,

00:06:24.732 --> 00:06:26.252
which was surprising, and so I said,

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:29.302
okay, I'm going to make
my own test audience.

00:06:29.302 --> 00:06:30.683
If this isn't part of the system,

00:06:30.683 --> 00:06:31.669
this isn't part of the process,

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:33.180
I'm going to make it part of the process,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.022
'cause that's how you
launch a successful product,

00:06:35.022 --> 00:06:37.439
and so I got 30 people,

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:38.919
half of whom were entrepreneurs

00:06:38.919 --> 00:06:40.639
and half of whom were not entrepreneurs.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:42.680
I felt like it would do
well with the entrepreneurs.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.181
I wasn't so sure about
the non-entrepreneurs,

00:06:45.181 --> 00:06:47.278
but it's good to get their
feedback, regardless.

00:06:47.278 --> 00:06:49.992
And I gave them the
120,000 word manuscript,

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:52.731
and they went through it.

00:06:52.731 --> 00:06:54.584
I got it back after them,
and they highlighted.

00:06:54.584 --> 00:06:56.563
I kept track of who highlighted what.

00:06:56.563 --> 00:06:58.296
Not so much concerned about

00:06:58.296 --> 00:07:01.255
this person's individual
opinion, but as a whole.

00:07:01.255 --> 00:07:03.039
These 30 people who reviewed it.

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:04.715
What were the most highlighted passages?

00:07:04.715 --> 00:07:06.354
What were the comments
that people liked the most?

00:07:06.354 --> 00:07:07.985
What were some parts that people found

00:07:07.985 --> 00:07:09.563
were really confusing?

00:07:09.563 --> 00:07:12.776
And, I took that to take
my 120,000 word manuscript

00:07:12.776 --> 00:07:15.237
down to the 65,000 words
that we have today.

00:07:15.237 --> 00:07:16.238
I cut out the chapters

00:07:16.238 --> 00:07:17.895
that people didn't resonate with as much.

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:20.625
I added chapter highlights

00:07:20.625 --> 00:07:22.482
from the quotes that people
underlined the most, right?

00:07:22.482 --> 00:07:24.864
Knowing how people react to your stuff

00:07:24.864 --> 00:07:26.472
can help you make a better product.

00:07:26.472 --> 00:07:29.723
And so, I'm super glad that
I went through that exercise,

00:07:29.723 --> 00:07:31.491
and also really grateful to the 30 people

00:07:31.491 --> 00:07:32.937
who reviewed it for me,

00:07:32.937 --> 00:07:35.748
'cause it gave me a lot of
insanely valuable information

00:07:35.748 --> 00:07:38.878
that I could take the book
from 120,000 words down to 65,

00:07:38.878 --> 00:07:42.247
and my agent wants to take that other

00:07:42.247 --> 00:07:43.533
55,000 words

00:07:43.533 --> 00:07:45.128
that we cut out and turn that into a book?

00:07:45.128 --> 00:07:48.438
No, that's not, that's
not, that's not a book.

00:07:48.438 --> 00:07:49.872
I'll throw away that book.

00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:50.745
If I'm going to do another book,

00:07:50.745 --> 00:07:52.305
it'll be somethin' totally different,

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:54.066
and I think that's part of
being an entrepreneur, too,

00:07:54.066 --> 00:07:55.583
and being good at what you do,

00:07:55.583 --> 00:07:57.166
is you create

00:07:57.166 --> 00:07:59.041
and throw away

00:07:59.041 --> 00:08:00.217
more than what most people

00:08:00.217 --> 00:08:01.498
even try to make.

00:08:01.498 --> 00:08:03.004
So many people just sit there with an idea

00:08:03.004 --> 00:08:04.632
and do nothing with it.

00:08:04.632 --> 00:08:06.856
Where the success entrepreneurs, they try.

00:08:06.856 --> 00:08:07.981
They try, they try, they try,

00:08:07.981 --> 00:08:09.555
they create, they create, they create,

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:11.139
they are prolific with their creations,

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:14.008
and not everything is going
to hit, and that's okay,

00:08:14.008 --> 00:08:16.352
and you take the best and you
keep moving forward with it,

00:08:16.352 --> 00:08:18.399
and the stuff that
didn't work, it's great,

00:08:18.399 --> 00:08:20.785
and I think it's good
to get into the practice

00:08:20.785 --> 00:08:25.343
of creating and throwing
away more than most people

00:08:25.343 --> 00:08:26.569
will even just create.

00:08:26.569 --> 00:08:30.253
Throw away more than most
people even try to create.

00:08:30.253 --> 00:08:32.209
You will fail your way to success,

00:08:32.209 --> 00:08:33.961
and so I'm really glad
I had my test audience

00:08:33.961 --> 00:08:36.192
because they gave me the
critical information,

00:08:36.192 --> 00:08:38.811
and then also, when I was going
through the back and forth

00:08:38.811 --> 00:08:42.129
with the editor, and the
publisher to say, "Hey, keep this,

00:08:42.129 --> 00:08:42.962
"don't keep this.".

00:08:42.962 --> 00:08:43.795
I had data.

00:08:43.795 --> 00:08:44.841
It's not just my opinion,

00:08:44.841 --> 00:08:46.027
'cause they had opinions, they'd say,

00:08:46.027 --> 00:08:47.533
"Oh, we don't like this.",
or "We like this.".

00:08:47.533 --> 00:08:51.316
Well listen, we tested it,
here's what people think.

00:08:51.316 --> 00:08:53.609
Real life people who are
entrepreneurs and not entrepreneurs

00:08:53.609 --> 00:08:54.686
who are going to read the book.

00:08:54.686 --> 00:08:56.096
Here's what they think,

00:08:56.096 --> 00:08:57.923
and we will use that test data

00:08:57.923 --> 00:09:00.255
to be able to fight for the parts

00:09:00.255 --> 00:09:02.290
that I really wanted to
incorporate into the book.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:03.180
So, really helpful.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.405
The number four thing I did right

00:09:04.405 --> 00:09:05.615
was I did it myself.

00:09:05.615 --> 00:09:07.145
Getting into this industry,

00:09:07.145 --> 00:09:10.373
I realized how many books
are not actually written

00:09:10.373 --> 00:09:11.293
by the author.

00:09:11.293 --> 00:09:13.433
There's ghost person with no credit,

00:09:13.433 --> 00:09:16.349
their audiobooks are
read by somebody else.

00:09:16.349 --> 00:09:18.086
It's just basically putting their name

00:09:18.086 --> 00:09:19.571
on the front of a book,

00:09:19.571 --> 00:09:22.543
and I definitely had a team who helped me,

00:09:22.543 --> 00:09:25.791
but most of the parts
that are in the book,

00:09:25.791 --> 00:09:27.880
and all of the parts
that are opinion pieces,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.385
it was me, I wrote it.

00:09:29.385 --> 00:09:30.985
I wrote it, it may have been edited,

00:09:30.985 --> 00:09:34.035
it may have been corrected,
some grammatical issues,

00:09:34.035 --> 00:09:35.510
but I wrote it, and it was one of the,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.607
actually the best things from the editors

00:09:37.607 --> 00:09:39.102
was they were, they
went through everything

00:09:39.102 --> 00:09:40.322
with a fine-tooth comb,

00:09:40.322 --> 00:09:41.605
and there's actually still a few errors

00:09:41.605 --> 00:09:43.166
and typos we found in the book,

00:09:43.166 --> 00:09:45.547
but they went through everything

00:09:45.547 --> 00:09:47.929
and corrected a lot of the grammar,

00:09:47.929 --> 00:09:48.834
which is great.

00:09:48.834 --> 00:09:50.772
I'm super grateful for
that, but I wrote it.

00:09:50.772 --> 00:09:53.504
It's my words, it's my
content, it's my meat,

00:09:53.504 --> 00:09:56.719
and I think as a result,
the product is better.

00:09:56.719 --> 00:09:58.659
I remember I was picking up an audiobook

00:09:58.659 --> 00:10:00.491
for a book I was really excited to read,

00:10:00.491 --> 00:10:01.572
I forget what it was,

00:10:01.572 --> 00:10:03.581
and then I found out that the person

00:10:03.581 --> 00:10:05.330
wasn't actually the person.

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:06.884
It was read by some actor.

00:10:06.884 --> 00:10:08.384
It's great, it's nothing
against the actor,

00:10:08.384 --> 00:10:09.686
it's just not the person.

00:10:09.686 --> 00:10:10.519
It's not the guy.

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:11.883
I want to hear from the guy,

00:10:11.883 --> 00:10:13.999
and so when we talked
about doing the audiobook,

00:10:13.999 --> 00:10:15.259
I insisted on doing it.

00:10:15.259 --> 00:10:16.491
Now, they wanted me, which was good.

00:10:16.491 --> 00:10:18.296
Most authors don't want to
put in the work to do it,

00:10:18.296 --> 00:10:19.783
and of course I'm going to do it.

00:10:19.783 --> 00:10:20.616
I need to do it.

00:10:20.616 --> 00:10:21.449
It's my book!

00:10:21.449 --> 00:10:23.467
It's my voice, I need to get it out there!

00:10:23.467 --> 00:10:26.529
So really, I think doing it myself

00:10:26.529 --> 00:10:29.004
was a super important part
of the book's success.

00:10:29.004 --> 00:10:30.486
And the number five thing I did right

00:10:30.486 --> 00:10:31.924
was I also got a team.

00:10:31.924 --> 00:10:34.195
As much as point number
four was I did it myself,

00:10:34.195 --> 00:10:35.478
I got a team for all the things

00:10:35.478 --> 00:10:36.921
that I wasn't good at,

00:10:36.921 --> 00:10:39.685
or I wasn't having a lot of success at,

00:10:39.685 --> 00:10:40.781
or I just needed help with, right?

00:10:40.781 --> 00:10:42.346
So, I had my two agents

00:10:42.346 --> 00:10:44.403
who made sure that a deal happened,

00:10:44.403 --> 00:10:46.037
and helped me find the right publisher

00:10:46.037 --> 00:10:47.041
with Penguin Random House,

00:10:47.041 --> 00:10:48.456
where we found the publisher who cared,

00:10:48.456 --> 00:10:50.868
who liked the message, who was on board,

00:10:50.868 --> 00:10:52.759
who supported the vision,
and that was important,

00:10:52.759 --> 00:10:54.177
'cause I don't know if
I would have gotten that

00:10:54.177 --> 00:10:57.549
on my own, and I didn't want
to publish without a publisher.

00:10:57.549 --> 00:11:00.095
I had people who helped
with some of the research

00:11:00.095 --> 00:11:01.065
on the book.

00:11:01.065 --> 00:11:02.985
I think about all of the
quotes that are in the book,

00:11:02.985 --> 00:11:06.911
and how many of them
Jason helped find for me,

00:11:06.911 --> 00:11:08.836
and just helped being an amazing filter

00:11:08.836 --> 00:11:10.823
so I didn't have to go out
and do all of the research.

00:11:10.823 --> 00:11:13.099
One of my friends, another
entrepreneur, Jennifer,

00:11:13.099 --> 00:11:15.403
helped me with a lot of the
interviews that I was doing.

00:11:15.403 --> 00:11:18.710
The third part of this book
is me interviewing people

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.172
who were also living their one word,

00:11:21.172 --> 00:11:22.458
and also plotted to their business,

00:11:22.458 --> 00:11:25.158
and went from $50,000
dollars full-time income

00:11:25.158 --> 00:11:27.440
up to $150 million dollars in revenue

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:29.379
off of their one word,

00:11:29.379 --> 00:11:32.361
and so sitting down and interviewing them.

00:11:32.361 --> 00:11:34.235
What I find is when
I'm doing an interview,

00:11:34.235 --> 00:11:38.042
I'm either in the present
and with that person,

00:11:38.042 --> 00:11:40.904
or I'm taking notes, and
have a hard time doing both,

00:11:40.904 --> 00:11:43.497
and so, Jen sat in with
me on hours and hours

00:11:43.497 --> 00:11:45.492
and hours of interview.

00:11:45.492 --> 00:11:48.213
Each one was three to
four hours, at least.

00:11:48.213 --> 00:11:49.348
She was takin' the notes,

00:11:49.348 --> 00:11:50.879
and she asked a couple
of great questions, too,

00:11:50.879 --> 00:11:53.091
and then she compiled the
notes and put it together,

00:11:53.091 --> 00:11:54.581
and it was really helpful,

00:11:54.581 --> 00:11:57.600
and so if the words
aren't my words, right?

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.389
If the words were Christopher Gavigan

00:11:59.389 --> 00:12:00.989
from the Honest Company,

00:12:00.989 --> 00:12:03.318
then she would write those words,

00:12:03.318 --> 00:12:06.032
and the words that were me, I wrote,

00:12:06.032 --> 00:12:07.163
which is most of the book.

00:12:07.163 --> 00:12:09.307
But, super helpful that we had that team.

00:12:09.307 --> 00:12:11.887
The guys who are on the audio side

00:12:11.887 --> 00:12:14.370
were paying so much attention to

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.268
the quality and the detail,

00:12:16.268 --> 00:12:18.297
and the audiobook came out fantastic

00:12:18.297 --> 00:12:20.228
in a way that I wouldn't
be able to do myself,

00:12:20.228 --> 00:12:22.713
and so I think it's really
important to understand

00:12:22.713 --> 00:12:24.078
where it is that you shine.

00:12:24.078 --> 00:12:25.234
I want to come up with a strategy,

00:12:25.234 --> 00:12:26.067
I want to come up with the ideas,

00:12:26.067 --> 00:12:27.084
I want to write the content

00:12:27.084 --> 00:12:29.042
that's coming from my voice, my opinions.

00:12:29.042 --> 00:12:32.274
I want to be the voice when
we're doing the audio recording.

00:12:32.274 --> 00:12:35.136
I want to be the guy on the
video, there's video bonuses

00:12:35.136 --> 00:12:37.221
for people that bought the ebook.

00:12:37.221 --> 00:12:39.418
I want to be that because
that's the most important

00:12:39.418 --> 00:12:40.543
use of my time.

00:12:40.543 --> 00:12:43.260
The other stuff, I'm not great at,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:44.879
and we can get it done a lot faster

00:12:44.879 --> 00:12:46.536
if I had people around me,

00:12:46.536 --> 00:12:48.785
and so I think that's important
to any successful launch

00:12:48.785 --> 00:12:49.912
of any product.

00:12:49.912 --> 00:12:51.230
Understand what you're really good at

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:52.519
and going all in on that,

00:12:52.519 --> 00:12:53.574
and surrounding yourself with people

00:12:53.574 --> 00:12:55.478
who can support you in the other projects.

00:12:55.478 --> 00:12:57.269
So, those were the five
things I did right,

00:12:57.269 --> 00:13:00.780
now let's get to the five
things that I did wrong.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:05.122
Up first is the bulk orders
versus the one, two, five.

00:13:05.122 --> 00:13:06.233
I really

00:13:06.233 --> 00:13:07.694
focused on

00:13:07.694 --> 00:13:08.644
bulk orders,

00:13:08.644 --> 00:13:10.930
'cause I was told to focus
on bulk orders, right?

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:13.927
I think you buy the one, but
then you can also buy 10,

00:13:13.927 --> 00:13:16.592
and then 25, forget what
the different packages were

00:13:16.592 --> 00:13:17.921
of books.

00:13:17.921 --> 00:13:22.304
And one of the bits of
advice that came back from

00:13:22.304 --> 00:13:23.921
one of the guys on my team was,

00:13:23.921 --> 00:13:26.323
"You should do a package for
two and a package for five.

00:13:26.323 --> 00:13:27.429
"If you buy two books, you get this,

00:13:27.429 --> 00:13:29.046
"you buy five books, you get this.",

00:13:29.046 --> 00:13:32.955
and the advice came while
we're already into the process,

00:13:32.955 --> 00:13:35.837
and the website was pretty
much built with all the offers,

00:13:35.837 --> 00:13:39.601
and I didn't take it, and I should've.

00:13:39.601 --> 00:13:41.784
Where I think a lot of people jumping

00:13:41.784 --> 00:13:44.543
from one book to 10 books is a big jump,

00:13:44.543 --> 00:13:46.980
but some people would buy two books,

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:48.396
and if you give them
an incentive to do it,

00:13:48.396 --> 00:13:50.845
or buy five books and give
them an incentive to do it,

00:13:50.845 --> 00:13:51.678
they would.

00:13:51.678 --> 00:13:53.445
So, most of the sales
that you end up making

00:13:53.445 --> 00:13:54.699
are going to be at the lower end,

00:13:54.699 --> 00:13:57.448
as opposed to people
buying 100 books at a time,

00:13:57.448 --> 00:13:59.432
and so giving some kind
of reward and incentive

00:13:59.432 --> 00:14:02.588
for somebody to buy one extra
book, or four extra books,

00:14:02.588 --> 00:14:04.442
could really make a difference.

00:14:04.442 --> 00:14:07.106
Number two is the lead time on the cover

00:14:07.106 --> 00:14:08.949
and on the creative.

00:14:08.949 --> 00:14:11.156
So, this is something
that I did not anticipate.

00:14:11.156 --> 00:14:13.140
One of the things I was really adamant on,

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.973
was I wanted each page
to be the end, so as I,

00:14:18.506 --> 00:14:19.745
let me load up any page.

00:14:19.745 --> 00:14:20.894
Random page.

00:14:20.894 --> 00:14:22.272
Oh, actually this one works.

00:14:22.272 --> 00:14:25.029
Okay, so you see how
here, it's at the top,

00:14:25.029 --> 00:14:27.954
there's a header, there's a
quote, and then there's content?

00:14:27.954 --> 00:14:30.537
And if I flip to the next page,

00:14:32.125 --> 00:14:33.049
it's not, right?

00:14:33.049 --> 00:14:35.185
It's in the middle, oh where am I, here.

00:14:35.185 --> 00:14:37.544
It's in the middle, and
there's a quote and content,

00:14:37.544 --> 00:14:40.330
and down on this side, it's
in the middle, quote, content.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:41.830
I wanted it to be at the top.

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:45.220
I wanted each page to be at the top.

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:46.784
I wanted the headline to be at the top,

00:14:46.784 --> 00:14:49.900
and then a quote, and then
each thing underneath,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:50.733
and

00:14:52.762 --> 00:14:53.793
we couldn't make it work.

00:14:53.793 --> 00:14:57.454
It was either I'm so
adamant about that structure

00:14:57.454 --> 00:14:59.349
that I walk away from the deal,

00:14:59.349 --> 00:15:01.682
because with the timeline set up,

00:15:01.682 --> 00:15:02.915
we couldn't make it work,
'cause I wasn't using

00:15:02.915 --> 00:15:06.574
the right template, and I'm
looking at 65,000 words,

00:15:06.574 --> 00:15:08.839
having to rewrite and
re-shift everything around.

00:15:08.839 --> 00:15:10.425
It wouldn't work for the timeline,

00:15:10.425 --> 00:15:12.258
and so that was super frustrating.

00:15:12.258 --> 00:15:16.002
Also, the cover, I'm
actually not a huge fan of.

00:15:16.002 --> 00:15:19.291
The cover was, the cover came
really late in the process.

00:15:19.291 --> 00:15:21.689
I, again, not knowing this process,

00:15:21.689 --> 00:15:23.779
assumed that we'd have
time to go back and forth

00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:26.707
and work with different
designers, and split test ideas,

00:15:26.707 --> 00:15:28.434
and it's not, it was
basically here's the idea

00:15:28.434 --> 00:15:31.248
from our art department,
what do you think?

00:15:31.248 --> 00:15:32.542
And the only room was for

00:15:32.542 --> 00:15:34.483
a couple minor tweaks and improvements,

00:15:34.483 --> 00:15:36.162
and then that's basically
what you go with.

00:15:36.162 --> 00:15:37.491
Well, we need an idea,

00:15:37.491 --> 00:15:40.013
we need a final version in two weeks.

00:15:40.013 --> 00:15:41.013
It's like...

00:15:42.334 --> 00:15:43.562
If you don't have a designer,

00:15:43.562 --> 00:15:45.922
if you don't have that skill in-house

00:15:45.922 --> 00:15:47.487
to go out and find somebody

00:15:47.487 --> 00:15:50.192
and get everything done,
and get it approved.

00:15:50.192 --> 00:15:51.911
We just ran out of time.

00:15:51.911 --> 00:15:54.268
That was on me, assuming that
we would have enough time.

00:15:54.268 --> 00:15:56.255
I assumed that the cover matters,

00:15:56.255 --> 00:15:57.793
the cover is important,

00:15:57.793 --> 00:16:00.108
the cover we would spend
a good amount of time on.

00:16:00.108 --> 00:16:01.889
Now, my publisher loves the cover.

00:16:01.889 --> 00:16:03.300
My agent loves the cover.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.098
I'm not a big fan of the cover.

00:16:06.098 --> 00:16:07.645
But, again, it's one of those things.

00:16:07.645 --> 00:16:10.319
Do I want to hold up
the launch of this book

00:16:10.319 --> 00:16:11.177
because of the cover?

00:16:11.177 --> 00:16:12.644
Am I going to walk away from the deal

00:16:12.644 --> 00:16:14.255
because of the cover?

00:16:14.255 --> 00:16:16.990
No, because the content
inside is really, really good,

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:19.357
and if I did it again in
terms of another book,

00:16:19.357 --> 00:16:21.466
I'd make sure I have a lot of lead time.

00:16:21.466 --> 00:16:23.588
I'd start designing the
cover from the beginning

00:16:23.588 --> 00:16:27.245
to make sure that that art is just as good

00:16:27.245 --> 00:16:30.281
as the content and the art
that's inside the book.

00:16:30.281 --> 00:16:31.266
I think it's important.

00:16:31.266 --> 00:16:32.913
The number four thing I did wrong

00:16:32.913 --> 00:16:35.537
was understanding the
importance of the credo.

00:16:35.537 --> 00:16:39.120
The credo has become
such an important part

00:16:40.296 --> 00:16:43.064
of the process, as I walk
people through it offline.

00:16:43.064 --> 00:16:44.238
So, the credo is the three things

00:16:44.238 --> 00:16:45.315
that make up your one word.

00:16:45.315 --> 00:16:46.390
So, you got your one word,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.665
and then the three things that add clarity

00:16:48.665 --> 00:16:50.606
make up the credo, and so I have believe,

00:16:50.606 --> 00:16:52.564
and in the believe, there's passion,

00:16:52.564 --> 00:16:54.234
you know, believe in what you're doing.

00:16:54.234 --> 00:16:57.395
There's confidence, which
is believe in yourself,

00:16:57.395 --> 00:16:58.228
and there's conviction,

00:16:58.228 --> 00:16:59.745
which is believe that
it's going to work out.

00:16:59.745 --> 00:17:02.007
So, believe, the one word, breaks down

00:17:02.007 --> 00:17:05.561
into passion, self-confidence,
and conviction,

00:17:05.561 --> 00:17:08.448
and that is a extremely valuable exercise

00:17:08.448 --> 00:17:11.471
for people to go through,
and it's in the book,

00:17:11.471 --> 00:17:14.264
but it's in the campaign
section of the book,

00:17:14.264 --> 00:17:16.569
where I realize in talking to people

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:18.190
and going through the process afterwards,

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:20.297
that really, it should part of core,

00:17:20.297 --> 00:17:23.310
because that understanding
is not just about marketing,

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.223
which is campaign, it's about you.

00:17:25.223 --> 00:17:26.453
It helps you with your life,

00:17:26.453 --> 00:17:27.842
and you make important decisions,

00:17:27.842 --> 00:17:30.508
and you live a better version
of what you want to do,

00:17:30.508 --> 00:17:35.278
and so it's buried more
into the campaign section

00:17:35.278 --> 00:17:37.381
instead of being more
upfront in the credo.

00:17:37.381 --> 00:17:39.275
So, if I had rewrite the content,

00:17:39.275 --> 00:17:41.771
that's one thing that I'd really focus on.

00:17:41.771 --> 00:17:44.997
Moving the credo earlier and
making it more important.

00:17:44.997 --> 00:17:46.748
It's something that I walk people through

00:17:46.748 --> 00:17:48.213
when I'm coaching them individually,

00:17:48.213 --> 00:17:50.413
the people on my team,

00:17:50.413 --> 00:17:52.964
but it's not nearly
important enough in the book.

00:17:52.964 --> 00:17:54.251
So, for those of you who have it,

00:17:54.251 --> 00:17:57.754
pay extra special attention
to the limited pages

00:17:57.754 --> 00:17:59.972
on the credo, because
it's really important.

00:17:59.972 --> 00:18:02.065
And number five is
underestimating the importance

00:18:02.065 --> 00:18:03.232
on leadership.

00:18:04.313 --> 00:18:06.298
I don't really talk about
leadership in this book.

00:18:06.298 --> 00:18:10.080
I never really thought of
it as a leadership book.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.276
It was more as a
self-leadership book, I guess

00:18:12.276 --> 00:18:14.831
you to manage yourself, and
the people that build the team

00:18:14.831 --> 00:18:18.147
and hire the right people, but not how to

00:18:18.147 --> 00:18:20.933
lead people with their one word,

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:23.144
and understand them to be better,

00:18:23.144 --> 00:18:24.685
and I've been getting a lot of interviews,

00:18:24.685 --> 00:18:26.762
a lot of requests, a lot
of speaking opportunities

00:18:26.762 --> 00:18:28.515
around leadership,

00:18:28.515 --> 00:18:31.658
and it's been so interesting
how people are using the book.

00:18:31.658 --> 00:18:35.721
So, we have CEOs and
entrepreneurs reading the book,

00:18:35.721 --> 00:18:38.161
and I think about their
team, and they realize,

00:18:38.161 --> 00:18:39.598
huh, my team should go through this.

00:18:39.598 --> 00:18:42.212
Everybody should find their
one word and find their credo,

00:18:42.212 --> 00:18:44.713
and if I understand
what their one word is,

00:18:44.713 --> 00:18:47.027
and I understand what their credo is,

00:18:47.027 --> 00:18:48.462
then I know how to push them

00:18:48.462 --> 00:18:50.546
to get the best thing that they want.

00:18:50.546 --> 00:18:51.855
So, as an example,

00:18:51.855 --> 00:18:53.427
any organization will have goals, right?

00:18:53.427 --> 00:18:55.293
We want to hit this amount in revenue.

00:18:55.293 --> 00:18:57.834
We all have these common goals
that we're shooting towards,

00:18:57.834 --> 00:18:59.622
but how we go and execute that,

00:18:59.622 --> 00:19:02.490
and how we motivate and push
people will be different,

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.055
so if our goal is to hit a
million dollars in revenue,

00:19:05.055 --> 00:19:06.754
and I've got somebody under me

00:19:06.754 --> 00:19:10.040
who they're responsible for
driving that project forward.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:11.463
What's their one word?

00:19:11.463 --> 00:19:13.994
If their one word is grit,

00:19:13.994 --> 00:19:18.046
then I have to push them
around gettin' the work done

00:19:18.046 --> 00:19:20.418
and not making excuses,
and let's go, go, go, go.

00:19:20.418 --> 00:19:22.316
If their one word is creativity,

00:19:22.316 --> 00:19:24.401
then I have to push them around coming up

00:19:24.401 --> 00:19:25.364
with creative ideas.

00:19:25.364 --> 00:19:26.831
I know that if I'm just telling them

00:19:26.831 --> 00:19:30.201
exactly what to do, somebody
whose one word is creativity

00:19:30.201 --> 00:19:33.163
is going to suffocate, 'cause
they need to be creative, right?

00:19:33.163 --> 00:19:35.471
We still have our goal of
what we want to accomplish,

00:19:35.471 --> 00:19:37.271
but the path to get there will change

00:19:37.271 --> 00:19:39.072
based on the people that
you have underneath you,

00:19:39.072 --> 00:19:40.660
and often what you'll find is

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:42.129
when you break down the credo,

00:19:42.129 --> 00:19:43.623
there's one part of that at least,

00:19:43.623 --> 00:19:45.564
that people are falling short of,

00:19:45.564 --> 00:19:48.720
so if self-confidence is
part of somebody's credo,

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:51.226
and you see them not being self-confident,

00:19:51.226 --> 00:19:53.908
you're given the tools to help push them.

00:19:53.908 --> 00:19:55.392
I believe a leader's role

00:19:55.392 --> 00:19:57.243
is to take somebody

00:19:57.243 --> 00:19:59.866
who you see has a lot of potential.

00:19:59.866 --> 00:20:01.450
They could be up here,
they're down here right now.

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:03.654
Your job is to help them close the gap.

00:20:03.654 --> 00:20:05.233
That's your job as a leader,

00:20:05.233 --> 00:20:07.691
is to see potential
and help close the gap.

00:20:07.691 --> 00:20:09.102
And so, by understanding somebody's

00:20:09.102 --> 00:20:10.850
one word and their credo,

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:13.856
they're giving you the tools to help them.

00:20:13.856 --> 00:20:16.044
'Cause if you think everybody
thinks like you, they don't.

00:20:16.044 --> 00:20:18.673
If you think everybody has the
same values and credo as you,

00:20:18.673 --> 00:20:19.730
they don't.

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:22.036
And so, understanding that,
and not just seeing everybody

00:20:22.036 --> 00:20:25.385
as you, and everybody as, well,
here's how I would solve it.

00:20:25.385 --> 00:20:26.905
That's not going to help everybody.

00:20:26.905 --> 00:20:28.269
There's going to be a ton of people

00:20:28.269 --> 00:20:29.843
who won't be able to solve the problem

00:20:29.843 --> 00:20:31.315
the exact same way you do,

00:20:31.315 --> 00:20:33.169
and that's where leaders often mess up.

00:20:33.169 --> 00:20:36.628
That's why a lot of,
you look at in baseball,

00:20:36.628 --> 00:20:39.676
some of the best baseball
hitters make terrible coaches,

00:20:39.676 --> 00:20:42.173
'cause they just try to
teach people how they do it,

00:20:42.173 --> 00:20:44.753
where what you need to
do is understand them

00:20:44.753 --> 00:20:47.149
to teach them in a way
that makes sense to them,

00:20:47.149 --> 00:20:50.559
and knowing what their
credo and their one word is.

00:20:50.559 --> 00:20:51.882
It's giving you the tools

00:20:51.882 --> 00:20:54.097
to say this is what I
feel is important to me,

00:20:54.097 --> 00:20:56.493
and so when you see them
falling short on those things,

00:20:56.493 --> 00:20:58.114
you know how to push them,

00:20:58.114 --> 00:21:00.431
and it's not pushing them for you.

00:21:00.431 --> 00:21:02.103
Yes, if they do well,
it helps the company,

00:21:02.103 --> 00:21:03.770
but it's pushing them for them.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.209
They've told you that
this is important to them.

00:21:05.209 --> 00:21:07.735
They've told you creativity,
or believe, or confidence

00:21:07.735 --> 00:21:09.048
is important to them,

00:21:09.048 --> 00:21:12.221
and so when they're not living
up to their own expectations,

00:21:12.221 --> 00:21:14.462
you know how to push them.

00:21:14.462 --> 00:21:17.137
So, it's been such an
important leadership tool,

00:21:17.137 --> 00:21:20.587
and a lot of that stuff isn't in the book.

00:21:20.587 --> 00:21:24.186
It almost makes sense as a sequel,

00:21:24.186 --> 00:21:26.720
although it's not the next
book that I want to write.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:31.025
So, anyway, I wish that
was somewhere in the book,

00:21:31.025 --> 00:21:33.722
